		{
			"id": "111263296371049402",
			"parentId": "111263214882731608",
			"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@bglw/111263296360702030",
			"author": {
				"name": "Liam Bigelow",
				"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@bglw",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/343/937/842/242/416/original/8905219901a4a606.jpeg"
			},
			"date": "2023-10-19T19:19:29.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> more broadly I guess. A lot of the parts I dislike around GQL are the pagination patterns, data loaders, and massive query mitigators. Which are all required when you’re essentially giving an end user access to a database. There are some contexts where you can skip all of those though, like build processes or server&lt;&gt;server communication, and in those contexts GQL’s core functionality can be really nice to use.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
